# 7i80HDT + 7i44 (sserial) + 7i49 motion, spindle, safety, and core panel I/O
# Logical allocation from mesa/current_pin_authority.csv; Excel workbooks are generated views.
#
# PLACEHOLDER PIN NAMES: every hm2_7i80.* name below is an UNVERIFIED PLACEHOLDER.
# The Mesa 7i80HDT is a bare-FPGA Ethernet host (hm2_eth) with three 50-pin daughter
# connectors (P1/P2/P3). Confirm the exact board tag (`hm2_7i80` expected) and the
# real analog-output (7i49 P1 pwmgen) and resolver (7i49 P1 resolver channel)
# pin structure from `readhmid` and
# `halcmd show pin hm2` before use. Axis feedback is RESOLVER via the 7i49,
# not quadrature encoder.
#
# Stack summary (2026-08-13 rev; P1/P2/P3 roles flipped vs. the 2026-08-06 rev
# once the actual 7i80hdt_rmsvss6_8.bin firmware was flashed and read back --
# see ../mesa/mesa_firmware_checklist.md):
#   P1: 7i49 (6 resolver channels + 6× ±10V analog outs)
#   P2: unused/spare (all bare-FPGA GPIO). Probe was previously wired to a bare
#       GPIO pin but has been moved to 7i84U-B (opto-isolated) - see fix #8.
#   P3: 7i44 sserial breakout - ONE sserial port with 8 channels
#        channel 0 -> 7i84U-A (existing 32/16 field I/O, hm2_7i80.0.7i84.0.0.*)
#        channel 1 -> 7i84U-B (limits, homes, drive-enables, relay-driven loads,
#                     probe, hm2_7i80.0.7i84.0.1.*)
#        channels 2-7 disabled in sserial_port_0="00xxxxxx" (reserved 7i44
#        RS-422 channels, not general field GPIO)
#
# All field I/O - limits, homes, E-stop monitor, drive-enables, spindle
# digital I/O, probe, and 100 VAC solenoid outputs - is on the two 7i84U cards
# (see field_7i84u.hal). The 7i80HDT motion HAL owns only motion-critical
# signals: resolvers and +/-10V velocity commands.

#####################################################################
# Resolver feedback channels (via Mesa 7i49 on P1)
#####################################################################
#
# Axis feedback on this machine is RESOLVER, not quadrature encoder.
#
# CONFIRMED (July 2026 on-machine photos):
#   Tamagawa Seiki TS2014N series SHAFT resolvers — Size 25, BRX type.
#   Mitsubishi/Mazak part: BKO-NC6062A.
#   Physical mounting: non-drive end of each ball screw, coupled via small flex coupling.
#   These are STANDALONE shaft resolvers, NOT motor-mounted (the HD-101/HD-81 DC servo
#   motors have integral tachogenerators for the TRAxxA velocity loop — completely
#   separate from the resolver position feedback path).
#
# Electrical spec (Tamagawa FA-SOLVER page, TS2014N141E26 row — COMPARISON DATA
# from a different suffix: the installed pickups read TS2014N 25 E … on X/Y,
# 2026-08-15 survey, docs/feedback_nameplate_survey_2026-08-15.md):
#   Excitation: 10 Vrms at 4.5 kHz, Primary R1-R2 (rotor, BRX type)
#   Transformation ratio K: 0.5 (no tolerance published on the Tamagawa page)
#   Rotor DC resistance:  121 ohms
#   Stator DC resistance:  69 ohms
#   Phase shift: -7.5 degrees;  Residual voltage: 15 mVrms max
#   Output type: check nameplate (Normal or Reverse)
#   If Reverse, position counts backwards — fix with negative scale setp below.
#
#   *** Full suffix matters. The values above are for TS2014N141E26 specifically.
#       The 2026-08-15 nameplate survey read TS2014N 25 E … on X and Y (Z
#       resolver nameplate still unread) — a DIFFERENT suffix, so none of the
#       absolutes above is a spec for this machine until the 25E datasheet is
#       obtained. PCW has warned on the LinuxCNC forum that some TS2014
#       variants (e.g. the E1/BRT family) are not compatible with the 7i49 at
#       all — that warning is live again for the 25E suffix. ***
#
# 7i49 default drive is ~2 Vrms and it expects ~1 Vrms on SIN/COS (2:1 ratio
# per the 7i49 manual). IF the installed 25E suffix shares K=0.5 (a 141E26
# comparison figure — installed suffix reads TS2014N 25 E…, 2026-08-15 survey;
# unconfirmed until the 25E datasheet is obtained), ~2 Vrms drive would return
# near 1 Vrms. Confirm on scope; do NOT add external dividers or 7i49HV
# hardware without a Mesa (PCW) review of the specific suffix.
#
# PLACEHOLDER: hm2_7i80.0.resolver.NN.* pin names unverified — confirm from
# `readhmid` / `halcmd show pin hm2` after loading firmware with resolver module.
#
# Pre-power ohmmeter checks (all values approximate — measure before energizing):
#   R1-R2 rotor winding: DC resistance ~121 ohms (141E26 comparison data —
#                        installed suffix reads TS2014N 25 E…, 2026-08-15
#                        survey; absolutes unconfirmed until the 25E datasheet
#                        is obtained. ZRO = 250 + j377 ohms at 4.5 kHz, same
#                        caveat)
#   S1-S3 (sin pair):    DC resistance ~69 ohms  (141E26 comparison data,
#                        same caveat; ZSS = 326 + j623 ohms at 4.5 kHz)
#   S2-S4 (cos pair):    same as S1-S3
#   The RATIO reasoning holds regardless of suffix: expect one distinctly
#   higher-DCR pair (rotor) and two matched lower pairs (stators).
#   Measure DC resistance FIRST and compare to the datasheet for the exact
#   suffix stamped on this resolver to confirm the variant before trusting
#   downstream signal-level predictions.
#   Any winding shorted to resolver case/shield = do not energize until resolved.

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Resolver position/velocity/index-divisor scales — PLACEHOLDERS
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Per the HostMot2(9) man page for the resolver component:
#   .scale          = machine units per RESOLVER ELECTRICAL revolution.
#                     NOT per motor rev and NOT per ballscrew rev unless the
#                     resolver is 1:1 coupled to the screw. On this machine the
#                     resolvers are flex-coupled to the ballscrew tail with no
#                     documented reduction, so one resolver electrical rev is
#                     expected to equal one ballscrew rev — but that must be
#                     verified by counting rawcounts against dial indicator
#                     travel over multiple full revolutions before it is trusted.
#   .velocity-scale = conversion factor between resolver rotation speed and
#                     machine velocity. Value 1 gives motor speed in RPS; it is
#                     an INDEPENDENT parameter from .scale, not automatically
#                     derived from it. To make .velocity report the same units
#                     as machine linear velocity, set velocity-scale to the
#                     same signed inches-per-revolution as .scale.
#   .index-divisor  = cycles per rev of the resolver (multi-speed resolvers
#                     have >1). Single-speed BRX is expected from the TS2014N
#                     family argument (the K = 0.5 figure is 141E26 comparison
#                     data — installed suffix reads TS2014N 25 E…, 2026-08-15
#                     survey), so 1 is expected; confirm from the 25E datasheet
#                     once obtained. The HostMot2 doc warns:
#                     'It is not appropriate to use this parameter for
#                     index-homing of axis drives.' — see the index-enable
#                     note further down.
#
# Derivation procedure (do before trusting these values):
#   1. Read the ballscrew lead off the screw or Mazak service data (inches or mm
#      per screw revolution; convert to inches for this inch config).
#   2. Confirm there is no reduction between screw and resolver (flex coupling
#      is 1:1; if any belt/pulley or gearbox is present, factor in its ratio).
#   2b. Count nulls per MECHANICAL revolution -- these detectors are MULTI-POLE.
#      The M2 maintenance manual classes the RT detector as a "multi-polar
#      resolver" (Table 14.3-1) and states grid points fall at each 1/n
#      revolution, n = number of poles (S6.7.1). HostMot2 .scale is per
#      ELECTRICAL revolution, so machine travel per electrical rev is the screw
#      lead divided by n. Do NOT assume n = 1. See
#      docs/resolver_commissioning.md Test 1.
#   3. Set RESOLVER_SCALE in the INI to signed inches-per-resolver-rev. The
#      sign selects axis direction: use a NEGATIVE value if the axis counts
#      backwards vs. the intended machine coordinate system.
#   4. Set RESOLVER_VELOCITY_SCALE to the same signed value so the .velocity
#      pin reports machine linear velocity in inches per second.
#   5. Verify by jogging a known distance with a dial indicator, then adjust.
#
# Only resolvers 00-02 exist (num_resolvers=3 in mazak_vqc_20_40.hal).
# Spindle feedback is unassigned; no encoder instance or receiver is active.
setp hm2_7i80.0.resolver.00.scale [JOINT_0]RESOLVER_SCALE
setp hm2_7i80.0.resolver.01.scale [JOINT_1]RESOLVER_SCALE
setp hm2_7i80.0.resolver.02.scale [JOINT_2]RESOLVER_SCALE

# 7i49 global excitation frequency.
# Confirmed resolvers (July 2026 photos): Tamagawa TS2014N series (Size 25 SHAFT resolvers),
# Mitsubishi/Mazak part BKO-NC6062A. Mounted at non-drive end of each ball screw via flex
# coupling — NOT motor-mounted. 141E26 comparison data: 10 Vrms at 4.5 kHz, K = 0.5.
# The 2026-08-15 nameplate survey (docs/feedback_nameplate_survey_2026-08-15.md) read
# the installed suffix as TS2014N 25 E… on X and Y — a DIFFERENT suffix from 141E26 —
# with the Z resolver nameplate still unread. Absolutes above are unconfirmed until
# the 25E datasheet is obtained.
#
# 7i49 selectable carrier frequencies per the 7i49 manual (RESMOD register): 2.5 / 5
# / 10 kHz. 4.5 kHz is not selectable, so the closest option is 5 kHz — about 11%
# above the 141E26 comparison figure (the installed 25E nominal is unknown). Tamagawa's FA-SOLVER page publishes NO frequency tolerance for
# TS2014N141E26, so 5 kHz operation is an assumption to VERIFY at commissioning by
# scoping RESDRV excitation and RESSIN/RESCOS amplitude and phase at rest and under
# motion — not to declare acceptable from a tolerance figure the datasheet doesn't
# provide.
#
# W2 does NOT help the axis channels: per the 7i49 manual, W2 down halves reference
# drive on channels 3/4/5 only. X/Y/Z live on channels 0/1/2 and W2 does not affect
# them. If measured SIN/COS at rest is well below the ~1 Vrms target, escalate to
# Mesa (PCW) review of the specific suffix before adding dividers or a 7i49HV.
setp hm2_7i80.0.resolver.excitation-khz 5

# Per-channel velocity scale (independent from position scale — see HostMot2
# resolver docs). Set to the same signed inches-per-resolver-rev as .scale so
# the .velocity pin reads machine linear velocity in in/s. Configured from INI.
setp hm2_7i80.0.resolver.00.velocity-scale  [JOINT_0]RESOLVER_VELOCITY_SCALE
setp hm2_7i80.0.resolver.01.velocity-scale  [JOINT_1]RESOLVER_VELOCITY_SCALE
setp hm2_7i80.0.resolver.02.velocity-scale  [JOINT_2]RESOLVER_VELOCITY_SCALE

# Index-divisor: cycles per rev of the resolver itself. TS2014N K=0.5 is a
# single-speed BRX resolver so 1 is expected — verify against the exact suffix
# datasheet. HostMot2 doc: 'It is not appropriate to use this parameter for
# index-homing of axis drives.' Homing on this machine is switch-based
# (HOME_USE_INDEX not set → NO), so the emulated index is not used for axis
# position homing.
setp hm2_7i80.0.resolver.00.index-divisor   [JOINT_0]RESOLVER_INDEX_DIVISOR
setp hm2_7i80.0.resolver.01.index-divisor   [JOINT_1]RESOLVER_INDEX_DIVISOR
setp hm2_7i80.0.resolver.02.index-divisor   [JOINT_2]RESOLVER_INDEX_DIVISOR

# Only axes 00/01/02 are resolvers. No resolver.03 instance is requested; a
# future fourth channel would require both a higher count and IDROM-proven pins.

net x-pos-fb        <= hm2_7i80.0.resolver.00.position
net x-vel-fb        <= hm2_7i80.0.resolver.00.velocity
net x-pos-rawcounts <= hm2_7i80.0.resolver.00.rawcounts
net x-resolver-fault <= hm2_7i80.0.resolver.00.error
net x-resolver-fault => resolver-fault-x.in1
net x-pos-fb        => joint.0.motor-pos-fb

net y-pos-fb        <= hm2_7i80.0.resolver.01.position
net y-vel-fb        <= hm2_7i80.0.resolver.01.velocity
net y-pos-rawcounts <= hm2_7i80.0.resolver.01.rawcounts
net y-resolver-fault <= hm2_7i80.0.resolver.01.error
net y-resolver-fault => resolver-fault-y.in1
net y-pos-fb        => joint.1.motor-pos-fb

net z-pos-fb        <= hm2_7i80.0.resolver.02.position
net z-vel-fb        <= hm2_7i80.0.resolver.02.velocity
net z-pos-rawcounts <= hm2_7i80.0.resolver.02.rawcounts
net z-resolver-fault <= hm2_7i80.0.resolver.02.error
net z-resolver-fault => resolver-fault-z.in1
net z-pos-fb        => joint.2.motor-pos-fb

# Index-enable nets INTENTIONALLY OMITTED.
# Homing on this machine is switch-based (HOME_SEARCH_VEL + HOME_LATCH_VEL,
# HOME_USE_INDEX defaults to NO). Wiring joint.N.index-enable to the resolver
# emulated index has no useful effect when HOME_USE_INDEX = NO and can be
# actively harmful: the HostMot2 doc warns the emulated index is not
# appropriate for index-homing of axis drives, and the pseudo-index position
# is not repeatable across LinuxCNC restarts.
#
# If a future homing design switches to index-homing, re-enable per axis with:
#   net x-index-enable    joint.0.index-enable <=> hm2_7i80.0.resolver.00.index-enable
#   net y-index-enable    joint.1.index-enable <=> hm2_7i80.0.resolver.01.index-enable
#   net z-index-enable    joint.2.index-enable <=> hm2_7i80.0.resolver.02.index-enable
# and set HOME_USE_INDEX = YES in each [JOINT_N] section — but only after
# reading the HostMot2 caution and confirming the resolver has an appropriate
# physical index. This TS2014N does not, and the emulated index from
# resolver.NN is explicitly not recommended for this use. Note the detector is
# MULTI-POLE (M2 maintenance manual Table 14.3-1), so the resolver null repeats
# n times per screw revolution and cannot identify a unique position within a
# turn -- switch-based homing stays mandatory.

# Spindle feedback — NOT a resolver channel.
# Confirmed (July 2026 analysis):
#   Tachogenerator: Tamagawa TGF-3D P402-Sx (from schematic 4143075313 sh3) — feeds the
#     FR-SX drive's own internal speed loop; LinuxCNC does not read this directly.
#   A separate machine-side A/B/Z encoder is believed present, but its model,
#     electrical format, and receiver/interface are not confirmed. The
#     confirmed rmsvss6_8 firmware has no Encoder module at all (readhmid
#     2026-08-13), and the active HostMot2 config uses num_encoders=0.
# No resolver.03 instance is created by the active three-channel configuration.
#
# TODO: after identifying the encoder, add a compatible receiver/daughter
# interface and IDROM-proven pins, then raise num_encoders and wire:
# net spindle-pos-fb   <= hm2_7i80.0.encoder.NN.position
# net spindle-vel-fb   <= hm2_7i80.0.encoder.NN.velocity
# net spindle-index-enable  motion.spindle-index-enable <=> hm2_7i80.0.encoder.NN.index-enable
# net spindle-revs     motion.spindle-revs
#
# spindle-at-speed is written EXCLUSIVELY by field_7i84u.hal from the FR-SX
# SPD-REACH discrete (7i84U-A IN13). Do NOT `sets spindle-at-speed` here or
# anywhere else - HAL rejects multiple writers on the same signal at load time.
# If the FR-SX SPD-REACH terminal is not yet wired, comment out the `net
# spindle-at-speed <= hm2_7i80.0.7i84.0.0.input-13` line in field_7i84u.hal
# and add `sets spindle-at-speed true` here as a BENCH-ONLY override (never
# leave both writers active).
# field_7i84u.hal also connects the signal to spindle.0.at-speed. LinuxCNC
# waits on that pin before the first feed after a spindle start/speed change
# and before spindle-synchronised motion. The FR-SX alarm signal similarly
# feeds spindle.0.amp-fault-in.

#####################################################################
# Analog servo outputs (7i49 P1 ±10V DACs, driven by pwmgen)
#####################################################################
#
# On the 7i49 the ±10V analog outputs are hostmot2 PWMGEN instances feeding
# onboard DACs -- there is no "analogout" pin. For a bipolar ±10V servo command:
#   output-type 2   (Up/Down mode, per Mesa 7i49 manual section "HostMot2 config":
#                    "The PWM generators should be set for UP/DOWN mode when
#                    used with HostMot2 firmware.")
#   scale 4000     (command in engineering units; see per-axis scale below)
#   pwm_frequency  24000 Hz (per 7i49 manual: below 24 kHz produces excessive
#                            output ripple; supported range 24 kHz to 500 kHz)
# In hostmot2, output-type 2 uses Out0=Up and Out1=Down. Type 4 (previously set
# here) is "locked antiphase" - WRONG for the 7i49. Refs:
#   https://linuxcnc.org/docs/html/man/man9/hostmot2.9.html (pwmgen output-type)
#   http://www.mesanet.com/pdf/parallel/7i49man.pdf (HostMot2 config section)
# The pwmgen .enable pin MUST be true or the output stays parked at zero.
#
# 7i49 P1 analog assignment (2026-08-13 rev):
#   AOUT0 = X axis velocity command  -> pwmgen.00
#   AOUT1 = Z axis velocity command  -> pwmgen.01
#   AOUT2 = Y axis velocity command  -> pwmgen.02
#   AOUT3 = FR-SX spindle velocity   -> pwmgen.03
#   AOUT4 = SPARE (no external analog orient reference exists on FR-SX;
#           orient is triggered by discrete ORCM1 command Y093, not analog)
#   AOUT5 = spare                    -> pwmgen.05 (spare)
#
# TODO: confirm pwmgen instance -> axis mapping and pin names from readhmid / show pin hm2.
# TODO: verify drive command polarity before enabling. A reversed command can cause runaway.

setp hm2_7i80.0.pwmgen.pwm_frequency 24000
# output-type 2 = Up/Down mode (Mesa 7i49 required config). Type 4 was WRONG
# (locked antiphase). Per hostmot2(9) man page:
#   type 1 = PWM+Dir      type 2 = Up/Down      type 3 = PDM+Dir
#   type 4 = Dir+PWM (locked antiphase, unsuitable for 7i49 DAC pipeline)
#
# Per-axis scale: per hostmot2(9) PWMGen doc, duty = value / scale, clipped to
# +/-1.0. On the 7i49 the pwmgen output drives a bipolar +/-10 V analog stage,
# so duty +/-1.0 = +/-10 V. With pid.output in machine units per second
# (velocity command from the outer position loop), setting scale equal to
# MAX_VELOCITY (in/s) maps pid.output = MAX_VELOCITY to +/-10 V ONLY IF the
# TRA drive is calibrated so 10 V equals MAX_VELOCITY shaft speed.
# That drive calibration is NOT yet measured; see docs/servo_commissioning.md
# steps 2 (zero-command null) and 3 (volts-per-speed). The [JOINT_N]OUTPUT_SCALE
# placeholder value in mazak_vqc_20_40.ini is **NOT** a safe electrical scale -
# LinuxCNC scaling means user-units-per-output-unit, and any non-measured value
# is guaranteed wrong. Bring-up policy: keep drives DISABLED (amp enable = 0)
# until OUTPUT_SCALE is measured per axis. Do not treat OUTPUT_SCALE=1.0 as
# 'safe' - see docs/superseded_claims_2026-08-06.md #23.
setp hm2_7i80.0.pwmgen.00.output-type 2    # X axis  -> 7i49 AOUT0
setp hm2_7i80.0.pwmgen.00.scale       [JOINT_0]OUTPUT_SCALE
setp hm2_7i80.0.pwmgen.01.output-type 2    # Z axis  -> 7i49 AOUT1
setp hm2_7i80.0.pwmgen.01.scale       [JOINT_2]OUTPUT_SCALE
setp hm2_7i80.0.pwmgen.02.output-type 2    # Y axis  -> 7i49 AOUT2
setp hm2_7i80.0.pwmgen.02.scale       [JOINT_1]OUTPUT_SCALE

net x-pos-cmd  <= joint.0.motor-pos-cmd
net x-pos-cmd  => pid.x.command
net x-pos-fb   => pid.x.feedback
# Velocity feedforward: derivative of commanded position fed to PID command-deriv input.
# joint.N.vel-cmd is the trajectory planner velocity output (LinuxCNC 2.9+).
# VERIFY: confirm pin name with `halcmd show pin joint.0` before enabling drives.
net x-traj-vel <= joint.0.vel-cmd
net x-traj-vel => pid.x.command-deriv
net x-vel-cmd  <= pid.x.output
net x-vel-cmd  => hm2_7i80.0.pwmgen.00.value
net x-enable   => pid.x.enable
net x-enable   => hm2_7i80.0.pwmgen.00.enable

net y-pos-cmd  <= joint.1.motor-pos-cmd
net y-pos-cmd  => pid.y.command
net y-pos-fb   => pid.y.feedback
net y-traj-vel <= joint.1.vel-cmd
net y-traj-vel => pid.y.command-deriv
net y-vel-cmd  <= pid.y.output
net y-vel-cmd  => hm2_7i80.0.pwmgen.02.value    # Y → pwmgen.02 (7i49 AOUT2)
net y-enable   => pid.y.enable
net y-enable   => hm2_7i80.0.pwmgen.02.enable

net z-pos-cmd  <= joint.2.motor-pos-cmd
net z-pos-cmd  => pid.z.command
net z-pos-fb   => pid.z.feedback
net z-traj-vel <= joint.2.vel-cmd
net z-traj-vel => pid.z.command-deriv
net z-vel-cmd  <= pid.z.output
net z-vel-cmd  => hm2_7i80.0.pwmgen.01.value    # Z → pwmgen.01 (7i49 AOUT1)
# Keep the MELDAS Z S-ON asserted briefly while the brake engages, but remove
# the LinuxCNC velocity command immediately. z-brake-release rises only after
# the torque-build delay and falls immediately on any disable; using it here
# prevents the PID/pwmgen from remaining live during z-drive-drop-delay's
# 100 ms S-ON shutdown hold.
net z-brake-release => pid.z.enable
net z-brake-release => hm2_7i80.0.pwmgen.01.enable

# Spindle analog speed command to Mitsubishi FR-SX (pwmgen.03 -> 7i49 AOUT3).
#
# SPINDLE COMMAND - architecture (A) chosen & implemented 2026-08-09 (C3):
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# RESOLVED: the FR-SX takes an unsigned 0-10 V magnitude + discrete FWD/REV
# (pg127). AOUT3 now carries spindle.0.speed-out-abs (see the net below); the
# spindle-output-permit remains held pending bench scaling/polarity. The A/B
# analysis retained below is the rationale (the "current net feeds signed"
# paragraph no longer applies):
#
# Two spindle-command architectures were considered:
#   (A) UNIPOLAR 0-10 V magnitude via pwmgen.03 + discrete FWD/REV outputs
#       on 7i84U-A OUT0/OUT1 (spindle-fwd / spindle-rev). This is the
#       classic Mitsubishi FR-SX pattern for freq-inverter spindles.
#   (B) BIPOLAR +/-10 V (sign encodes direction) via pwmgen.03 alone; no
#       FWD/REV discretes.
#
# The current net feeds spindle.0.speed-out (SIGNED) directly into
# pwmgen.03.value AND asserts OUT0/OUT1 direction discretes. On reverse
# spindle (M4), speed-out goes NEGATIVE, so AOUT3 outputs negative voltage
# to a drive whose FWD/REV state is already asserted on the discrete
# inputs. If the FR-SX is (A), negative AOUT3 either latches an alarm or
# is clipped to zero - reverse spindle produces no motion. If the FR-SX
# is (B), the discrete FWD/REV outputs are redundant and may fight the
# analog sign, and either the drive or the discrete-input wiring must be
# removed.
#
# ACTION REQUIRED BEFORE FIRST SPINDLE ROTATION:
#   1. Bench-measure the FR-SX speed-reference input polarity per its
#      manual: connect a variable DC source to the AOUT3 terminal with
#      the drive isolated from mains, run FWD/REV at the digital inputs,
#      and observe whether the drive expects unsigned magnitude or signed
#      voltage. Document the answer in docs/frsx_orient_model.md.
#   2. Choose architecture (A) OR (B), not both.
#   3. If (A): insert an 'abs' component between spindle.0.speed-out and
#      pwmgen.03.value so AOUT3 never goes negative:
#          loadrt abs count=1
#          addf abs.0 servo-thread
#          net spindle-speed-signed  <= spindle.0.speed-out
#          net spindle-speed-signed  => abs.0.in
#          net spindle-speed-mag     <= abs.0.out
#          net spindle-speed-mag     => hm2_7i80.0.pwmgen.03.value
#      Keep OUT0/OUT1 discrete FWD/REV as-is.
#   4. If (B): remove spindle-fwd / spindle-rev writers in field_7i84u.hal
#      and delete the OUT0/OUT1 nets. Keep the direct signed net below.
#
# field_7i84u.hal gates FWD, REV, RUN, and this pwmgen enable, while
# atc_orient.hal gates ORCM1, through spindle-motion-permit. That dynamic
# permit requires the static spindle-output-permit hold (initialized FALSE),
# watchdog health, estop-ok, machine-on, servo-ready, and no indicated spindle
# fault. Clearing the static hold is the only supported commissioning path.
#
# output-type 2 = Up/Down mode (7i49 requires this on all four analog channels).
# scale = max spindle RPM at full-scale output. Per [SPINDLE_0]MAX_FORWARD_VELOCITY
# in the INI, currently 4000 RPM.
setp hm2_7i80.0.pwmgen.03.output-type 2
setp hm2_7i80.0.pwmgen.03.offset-mode 0
setp hm2_7i80.0.pwmgen.03.scale       [SPINDLE_0]OUTPUT_SCALE
# OPEN-LOOP spindle placeholder: speed command goes directly to pwmgen without
# PID. pid.s is loaded (mazak_vqc_20_40.hal) but unconnected - pid.s.enable
# defaults to false so it outputs 0 harmlessly. Do NOT close the loop until
# the HOLD above is cleared.
#
# ARCHITECTURE (A) implemented 2026-08-09 (C3 resolved per pg127): feed the
# built-in UNSIGNED magnitude spindle.0.speed-out-abs into pwmgen.03 so AOUT3
# never goes negative; spindle direction is the discrete FWD/REV outputs
# (7i84U-A OUT0/OUT1). The spindle-output-permit stays held - bench-verify the
# FR-SX scaling (10 V = max RPM) and SRN/SRI polarity/sink-source before
# clearing the permit.
net spindle-speed-cmd <= spindle.0.speed-out-abs
net spindle-speed-cmd => hm2_7i80.0.pwmgen.03.value
# The safe RUN signal is the output of the commissioning gate and therefore
# enables the analog channel only when the physical RUN output is also allowed.
net spindle-run-output => hm2_7i80.0.pwmgen.03.enable

# 7i49 AOUT4 and AOUT5 are physically spare. They do not have HostMot2
# pwmgen instances in the active num_pwmgens=4 configuration: four instances
# means pwmgen.00 through pwmgen.03, exactly X/Z/Y/spindle.
#
# Earlier revisions of this file reserved AOUT4 as an 'FR-SX orient reference'.
# That was wrong. The Mitsubishi FR-SX (SJ / FREQROL-SF family) spindle drive
# does NOT accept an external analog orient reference. Per the FR-SF and
# MDS-CH orient documentation, orient is triggered by a discrete NC-to-drive
# command (ORC / ORCM1) with the target position selected either by:
#   - a machine-side magnetic sensor (one-point orientation, MAGSENSOR on CN6)
#   - a machine-side encoder (multi-point 4096p orientation on CN6)
#   - the motor-built-in PLG detector (multi-point orientation on CN5)
# Selection is by drive parameters (#41 OSL or SP037 enco/nsno/plgo bits).
# The orient position is fixed by the sensor magnet or encoder Z-phase and
# ratio, NOT commanded from LinuxCNC in real time.
#
# In this retrofit, orient is issued via 7i84U-A TB3 OUT4 (Y093 ORCM1.M)
# to the FR-SX; arrival is monitored via the ORA feedback path
# (see atc_orient.hal and mazak_orient.comp).
#
# Keep num_pwmgens=4 while the spindle analog command uses pwmgen.03. Reducing
# the count to 3 would remove the spindle instance, not merely a spare output.

#####################################################################
# P2: unused (no bare-FPGA I/O bindings)
#####################################################################
#
# The stack uses P1 (7i49 analog+resolver) and P3 (7i44 sserial). P2 is
# reserved/spare, confirmed bare GPIO (gpio.024-gpio.047) by readhmid
# 2026-08-13; nothing in this configuration binds a P2 pin.
#
# Earlier drafts wired the Renishaw MP-3 probe SKIP1 signal to bare GPIO
# for latency reasons. That path was REMOVED because the 7i80HDT's bare-GPIO
# pins are 3.3 V logic without opto-isolation: exposing them to 24 V field
# wiring risks destroying the FPGA. The probe is now routed through the
# 7i84U-B card (isolated 24 V input) - see field_7i84u.hal (input-15) and
# mesa/current_pin_authority.csv row PROBE_SKIP1. End-to-end latency and
# stopping error must be measured; neither the servo period nor SSLBP baud rate
# proves a fixed input-age bound. No primary Mesa or LinuxCNC source states a
# '1 ms sserial per 2.5 MBd' figure. Record trigger-to-motion.probe-input
# latency and probing stopping error at each approved feed before accepting
# this path.
# See <https://linuxcnc.org/docs/html/man/man9/hostmot2.9.html>.
#
# Drive faults, drive-enables, limits, homes, E-stop monitor, probe, and all
# field solenoid outputs are routed via the 7i84U cards - see field_7i84u.hal.
